messages.text:
Yes. Fix 3 is now the first fix to get the "real rendering complexity" report you asked for. It will also be the template for the other fixes.

What it measures, each time your finger pins the menu panel, original vs fix:
1. how many touch listeners get added and removed (the fix's whole point is "set up once" instead of again and again);
2. how much style, layout, repaint and compositing work the page does per pin and per frame while you hold it;
3. how many GPU layers the panel uses and how much memory;
4. how much script runs per pin;
5. whether every touch move is still handled the same way.

How: the fix's owner (fix-nav-morph) runs it in Chrome and the iPhone Simulator, with no phone and no heat. The measurement planning agent supplies the report's layout. It counts per pin, not per time window; that lesson came from this morning's countdown trial.

What happens with the result:
- If it shows the fix removes real work per pin and adds no new cost, I'll come back to you with the option to land fix 3 on that evidence. That changes the phone-test rule we set for it, so it's your call.
- If it shows no difference, I'll tell you plainly and we'll decide between a better phone test and parking it.
